                <pb facs="0379" n="273" sameAs="frus:frus1891/pg273"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Egan to Mr. Blaine</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1891-12-19"><hi rendition="#italic">December 19, 1891</hi>.</date>
                        (Received December 20.)</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Egan reports that the legation is still being watched by some twenty
                    policemen, whose attitude he describes as most offensive, and that his son, an
                    employé of the legation, was, for having gone out from the legation at 5 p.m.,
                    seized by the arm by a detective, who detained him on the street and called two
                    regular policemen to his aid. He adds that he was allowed to proceed as soon as
                    he was recognized.</p>
            </div>
